# AUTOLAB DIGITAL SHOWROOM

### Product Blueprint & Decision Freeze

**Version 0.1 — October 2026**

---

## 1. PURPOSE OF THIS DOCUMENT

This document defines the first version of the **AutoLab Digital Showroom**.

It is intended to establish a shared understanding between AutoLab and the project creator before design and development begin.

The purpose is not to define every future capability of the platform.

It is to establish:

- what we are building;
- why we are building it;
- who it is for;
- what the first version must accomplish;
- what it will not attempt to do;
- how the experience will support AutoLab's existing sales process;
- what the project includes commercially;
- and where future expansion can occur.

The product will be developed as a **bespoke digital showroom experience for AutoLab**, rather than as a generic automotive application.

---

# 2. THE BUSINESS PROBLEM

AutoLab's customers often know that they want to transform their vehicle interior, but they struggle to visualise the finished result before the physical work begins.

This is particularly important because AutoLab's work is highly personalised.

The customer may need to make decisions around:

- material;
- colour;
- accent thread;
- extent of personalisation;
- and the overall visual composition of the interior.

At present, much of this decision-making ultimately requires the customer to visit the showroom.

The Digital Showroom is intended to improve this experience by allowing the customer to **see and participate in the proposed transformation before committing to the physical work.**

This aligns directly with AutoLab's stated philosophy:

> **AutoLab should offer an experience rather than feeling like a transaction.**

---

# 3. PRODUCT MISSION

### Mission

**Give AutoLab customers a way to experience, configure and visualise their proposed interior transformation before the physical work begins.**

The product should make the customer feel involved in creating their interior rather than simply purchasing an upholstery service.

---

# 4. PRODUCT VISION

The AutoLab Digital Showroom should feel less like an ordinary website and more like entering a digital version of AutoLab's design process.

The customer should be able to:

**Select → Configure → Visualise → Review → Engage AutoLab**

The experience should communicate the same qualities AutoLab wants its physical work to communicate:

- precision;
- luxury;
- craftsmanship;
- personalisation;
- transformation;
- confidence.

The technology exists to enhance AutoLab's craftsmanship, not to replace the showroom.

---

# 5. CUSTOMER

### Primary customer

Existing and prospective AutoLab customers considering premium vehicle interior customisation and reupholstery.

### Initial customer profile

Customers with premium/luxury vehicles who are interested in substantial interior transformation rather than minor repairs.

AutoLab has indicated that premium luxury vehicles provide the strongest platform for demonstrating its capabilities because they allow the greatest degree of personalisation.

---

# 6. PRODUCT CATEGORY

The AutoLab Digital Showroom is:

**A premium interactive automotive interior visualisation and configuration experience.**

It is not:

- an automotive marketplace;
- an e-commerce platform;
- a vehicle marketplace;
- a CRM;
- a workshop management system;
- an ERP;
- a booking platform;
- a payment platform;
- a vehicle inventory database;
- or a generic AI car-design application.

The distinction is important.

**The product exists to sell the AutoLab transformation experience.**

---

# 7. DECISION FREEZE

The following decisions define the initial product boundary.

## 7.1 Mission

Allow AutoLab customers to visualise and participate in the design of their vehicle's interior transformation before physical work begins.

## 7.2 Primary experience

The customer configures a premium vehicle interior and immediately sees the visual result.

## 7.3 Initial vehicle

The first experience will be built around a **Mercedes-Benz S-Class** as the leading vehicle platform.

The exact S-Class generation/model and physical reference vehicle must be confirmed by AutoLab before final 3D asset production begins.

The system should be architected so that additional vehicles can be added later without rebuilding the entire product.

## 7.4 Initial configuration model

The first configuration experience will centre around:

1. Material
2. Colour
3. Accent thread
4. Interior composition/visual configuration

Additional configuration options may be introduced where AutoLab confirms that they are genuinely offered and can be represented accurately.

## 7.5 Primary material

Leather is the primary material category.

Fabric may be supported where appropriate based on AutoLab's actual catalogue.

## 7.6 Primary commercial purpose

The experience should generate stronger customer engagement and encourage the customer toward AutoLab's existing showroom/sales process.

The Digital Showroom is **not intended to replace the physical showroom.**

---

# 8. CORE CUSTOMER JOURNEY

## STEP 1 — ENTER THE SHOWROOM

The customer enters an AutoLab digital environment designed around premium automotive interior transformation.

The experience immediately communicates that this is about creating an interior rather than simply browsing a service list.

---

## STEP 2 — SELECT THE VEHICLE

The first release presents the agreed S-Class model.

Future vehicles can be introduced through the same underlying architecture.

---

## STEP 3 — CONFIGURE THE INTERIOR

The customer begins making design choices.

The first release focuses on:

**Material → Colour → Accent Thread**

The interface should make these decisions visual rather than text-heavy.

---

## STEP 4 — VISUALISE

The selected configuration is represented directly on the 3D vehicle interior.

The customer can interact with the interior and inspect the proposed transformation.

The visual result is the core value of the product.

---

## STEP 5 — REVIEW

The customer can review the selected configuration as a coherent design rather than a collection of individual choices.

The system should provide a configuration summary containing the relevant selections.

---

## STEP 6 — ENGAGE AUTOLAB

The customer is encouraged toward the next real-world step:

**Visit AutoLab / Contact AutoLab / Request a consultation**

The configuration information can accompany the enquiry so that AutoLab has context before continuing the conversation.

---

# 9. THE "WOW" PRINCIPLE

AutoLab has identified the strongest visual reaction as the transformation of basic interiors into more vibrant compositions.

Therefore, the Digital Showroom should not behave like a technical configurator where the customer simply changes dropdown values.

The experience should communicate:

**"This is what your interior could become."**

The transformation itself is part of the product.

Where technically and commercially justified, before/after presentation can be incorporated into the experience.

However, photorealistic before/after animation is not considered a mandatory requirement until the actual implementation cost and asset requirements are established.

---

# 10. 3D EXPERIENCE

The 3D interior is the central production asset of the Digital Showroom.

This is also the largest technical and commercial risk in the project.

The research phase identified three broad asset scopes:

- individual component;
- complete interior/cockpit;
- complete vehicle.

A complete vehicle is unnecessary for the initial product.

### Decision

The first release will focus on the **vehicle interior**, not the complete exterior vehicle.

The objective is to achieve enough visual fidelity to convincingly communicate AutoLab's interior transformation capabilities without paying the complexity cost of building an entire digital vehicle.

---

# 11. MATERIAL SYSTEM

The material system will be built around AutoLab's actual offerings.

### Confirmed primary material

**Leather**

### Additional confirmed category

**Fabric**, where applicable.

The final launch catalogue will use AutoLab-approved material names, colours and available options.

No material, stitching pattern, finish or colour will be presented as an AutoLab offering merely because it is technically possible.

This is important because the research phase identified several possible material/finish combinations as assumptions rather than verified AutoLab offerings.

---

# 12. CONFIGURATION ENGINE

The configuration engine controls the relationship between the customer's choices and the visual output.

At minimum:

**Vehicle**
↓
**Material**
↓
**Colour**
↓
**Accent Thread**
↓
**Visual Configuration**

The architecture should allow additional configuration dimensions to be added later without redesigning the entire system.

Examples of future dimensions may include additional interior components, material categories, stitching options or carbon-fibre elements.

These are expansion possibilities, not V1 commitments.

---

# 13. CONFIGURATION OBJECT

Every customer configuration should be treated as a structured object.

Conceptually:

**Configuration**

- Vehicle
- Material
- Colour
- Accent Thread
- Selected Components
- Visual State
- Configuration Reference
- Customer Information, where voluntarily provided
- Enquiry/Contact State

This allows AutoLab to move from:

> "A customer saw something on the website"

to:

> "A customer created this particular interior configuration."

---

# 14. SALES HANDOFF

The Digital Showroom does not replace AutoLab's existing sales process.

The intended relationship is:

**Digital experience → Customer interest → Configuration → AutoLab showroom/contact → Physical quotation → Physical work**

This reflects AutoLab's current preference for bringing customers into the showroom rather than attempting to complete the entire sales process through messaging.

Pricing is therefore **not automatically required in V1.**

AutoLab has confirmed that quotations vary according to factors including:

- the current condition of the vehicle;
- the extent of personalisation;
- and the customer's desired scope.

Therefore the Digital Showroom should not create false pricing certainty.

---

# 15. PRICING PRINCIPLE

### V1 will not be positioned as an e-commerce checkout system.

The Digital Showroom is a visualisation and sales-enablement tool.

Where a customer requires a quotation, AutoLab will assess the vehicle and proposed work through its normal process.

This preserves AutoLab's ability to price bespoke work appropriately.

---

# 16. DATA & BACKEND PRINCIPLE

The product does not require a large automotive database.

The system should store only the information required to support:

- configurations;
- enquiries;
- configuration references;
- relevant customer/contact information;
- and operational analytics where justified.

The research phase similarly found no strong justification for a large account ecosystem, e-commerce payment system or heavy backend for the first release.

### Principle

**Store configurations and leads, not the entire automotive industry.**

---

# 17. ADMINISTRATION

A lightweight administrative capability may be included where it provides genuine operational value.

Potential functions include:

- viewing enquiries;
- viewing saved configurations;
- managing available configuration options;
- updating material information;
- viewing basic usage information.

A complex CMS or enterprise administration system is outside V1.

---

# 18. PRODUCT ARCHITECTURE

The product will be divided into clear modules.

### Module 01 — Experience Shell

The main digital showroom interface.

### Module 02 — Vehicle / Interior Viewer

The interactive 3D environment.

### Module 03 — Configuration Engine

Controls customer selections and configuration state.

### Module 04 — Material System

Controls material and colour presentation.

### Module 05 — Configuration Summary

Converts the customer's design into a structured summary.

### Module 06 — Lead / Enquiry

Transfers customer interest into AutoLab's sales process.

### Module 07 — Asset Layer

Contains the 3D models, textures and supporting visual assets.

### Module 08 — Lightweight Administration

Only where operationally justified.

### Module 09 — QA / Performance

Ensures the experience works reliably across supported devices and browsers.

---

# 19. TECHNOLOGY PRINCIPLES

The product will use established web technologies rather than attempting to create a proprietary 3D engine.

The expected architecture may include:

- modern web application framework;
- WebGL-based 3D rendering;
- Three.js / React Three Fiber or equivalent;
- optimised 3D assets;
- responsive frontend;
- lightweight backend/database where required;
- WhatsApp/contact integration where appropriate;
- cloud deployment.

The exact implementation stack is an engineering decision to be made after the product specification is frozen.

---

